 2. ⚠️ Time capsule of this post-processable week 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Via X 

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Talk about timing! After a remarkable string of tech failures disrupted American life, the federal government will conduct a nationwide broadcast test of its emergency alert system — you know the sound — today at 2:20 p.m. ET. The test will air on radio, broadcast, cable and satellite TV.

 The test timing is a coincidence. It was announced in September, long before yesterday's outages at JPMorgan Chase and United Airlines, last week's Microsoft meltdown and the FAA's longest nationwide ground stop since 9/11. But that hasn't stopped a wave of online conspiracy theories, including viral claims that today's test signals the start of a 90-day nationwide blackout.

 FEMA's tweet : "We've heard a lot of rumors about [today's] EAS [Emergency Alert System] test. 📺📻 Let's clear things up. It lasts 1 minute, and no action is required. No blackouts, no secret emergencies. Just our legally mandated, routine test on TV and radio at approximately 2:20 PM ET." Via X 🔌 What happened yesterday: Thousands of bank customers reported difficulty accessing online accounts and transferring money through Zelle after a technical issue at JPMorgan Chase.

 Chase blamed a software bug and said transactions were back to normal last night. Separately, United Airlines resolved a "widespread system outage" that delayed departing flights for about 90 minutes. The airline said there was no indication of a cybersecurity-related issue but didn't disclose the cause.

 Via X 📺 The intrigue: Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin told Fox News' Sean Hannity last night that federal cybersecurity officials have found no evidence linking the outages to a coordinated cyberattack.

 Mullin also speculated that AI could be behind the outages. He cited an unnamed person in the tech industry who suggested that AI programs writing and changing their own code could overwhelm older systems. He offered no hard evidence. Today's Wall Street Journal 🫨 A Wall Street Journal front-page story today features a stark obit for the go-go AI market: "For three years, investors have minted money from the AI boom without having to make hard choices. Nearly every stock tied to what is projected to be the largest infrastructure spending spree in U.S. history zoomed higher, from chip makers to power companies to obscure equipment makers to cloud-computing companies of all sizes."

 "That 'no-brainer' era is over," the article continues. "A punishing bout of volatility stretching back to the Nasdaq's recent peak on Oct. 6, through the election's aftermath and Jensen Huang's stunning exit as CEO of Nvidia, has fundamentally rewired the AI trade. ... The AI trade is vulnerable from a dizzying number of angles." Keep reading (gift link). 

 
 
