 3. ⚡ U.S. allies fear &quot;Yalta II&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump inspects an honor guard today as he disembarks from Air Force One at Ninoy Aquino International Airport in Manila, Philippines, for the ASEAN summit. Photo: Mark Schiefelbein/AP President Trump's trilateral summit with Xi Jinping and Vladimir Putin has the makings of an unpredictable spectacle — and that's exactly what has U.S. allies anxious, Axios' Barak Ravid writes. 

 Why it matters: Trump's stated goal for the first-ever summit between the nations' three leaders is to start a discussion on nuclear disarmament. 

 But European and Western diplomats fear he could go off script and take positions that undermine Ukraine or Taiwan — or appear to side with their chief adversaries. 🎨 The big picture: Many U.S. allies are nervous that the meeting will project an image of the three leaders scheming to carve up the earth into spheres of influence. 

 Some of them invoked "Yalta II" — referring to the 1945 summit between the U.S., Great Britain and the Soviet Union that helped shape postwar Europe. Trump also floated a possible meeting with North Korean leader Kim Jong Un. A painting by Russian artist Alexey Sergienko depicting the three leaders ahead of their meeting in Shenzhen, China. Photo: Andrei Bok/Reuters 👀 Behind the scenes: The Trump-Xi-Putin summit was a topic of closed-door talks among European leaders at a meeting in Dublin last week, according to a European diplomat.

 Some of them raised concerns about what Trump might say about the war in Ukraine, the diplomat said. Keep reading . 

 
 
