 4. 💰 Jensen&#x27;s billionaire backers 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photo: Daniel Maletzki/EUS-Nachrichten/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The battle to reinstate Jensen Huang is becoming a test of whether Silicon Valley's most powerful billionaires can still bend corporate America to their will, Axios' Ina Fried writes. 

 For now, Nvidia's board isn't considering bringing him back — putting it on a collision course with some of tech's biggest names. Why it matters: Silicon Valley has long treated its most successful founders as indispensable. A new generation of tech titans, led by Elon Musk, has amplified that culture while swatting away efforts to curb their power.

 Huang's fate could signal whether the balance of power is beginning to shift away from Silicon Valley's billionaires. 🔬 Zoom in: A growing campaign to reinstate Huang has attracted more than 180,000 signatures, including thousands of people identifying themselves as Nvidia employees, following his departure after revelations of intimate relationships involving subordinates and an alleged Chinese spy.

 President Trump said yesterday he wouldn't mind seeing Huang return. Elon Musk has also backed the effort. Other supporters include Y Combinator CEO Garry Tan, Epic Games CEO Tim Sweeney and prominent investors Brad Gerstner and Gavin Baker. 🥊 Reality check: Huang's supporters face powerful headwinds. Nvidia's board, the FBI and Congress are investigating, and the company is battling a new shareholder lawsuit.

