 7. 🎒 International student slump 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Fewer international students are enrolling at American universities — costing the economy billions of dollars and hurting college towns, Axios' Josephine Walker writes from data released by the Institute of International Education . 

 Why it matters: International students typically pay higher tuition than their domestic counterparts. 🧮 By the numbers: New international student enrollment at U.S. colleges and universities fell 18% during the 2025–26 academic year.

 Overall international enrollment dropped 3%. The decline is hitting graduate programs hardest. International grad student numbers fell 13%, while undergraduate enrollment barely budged, slipping just 0.3%. Share this story . 

 
 
